  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved regulatory framework and implementation mechanisms to promote equal opportunity for women in the world of work including in entry, mid and higher management levels in EBMOs, firms and trade unions, and for migrant workers</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Migrant domestic workers, often women, make a significant share of international migrant workers in Lebanon. They are part of the important care workforce that is essential for ensuring the well-being of the family, community and society.  Their working conditions are generally characterized by multiple decent work deficits. The situation has worsened during the COVID-19 pandemic, economic crisis and the recent war. Other care workers such as healthcare workers and care workers in education (e.g. childcare workers) also faced challenges in accessing decent work. 

 

Following the adoption of the ILO Resolution on Decent Work and the Care Economy, meetings were held with trade unions to discuss areas of work to strengthen their action in promoting decent work. The re-designed RBSA intervention in Lebanon will support constituents in their efforts to promote decent work in the care economy. The RBSA intervention also seeks to support employers&#x2019; organization and the government in initiating action in the care economy to promote gender equality and decent work.</narrative>
  </description>
